            <section class="inner-wrapper">
                <h1>My Emotional Regulation</h1>
                <h2>Make responses automatic</h2>
                <h3>Calm practice → mild stress → real-life stress → catch the old pattern → redirect → notice success
                </h3>
            </section>
            <section class="inner-wrapper">
                <h3>Stage 1 — Practice when I'm completely calm</h3>
                <div>
                    <img src="images/jesus-with-child.webp" alt="Jesus with a little child">
                    <p>This is where you build the pathway.

                        Once or twice a day, spend 2–3 minutes rehearsing.

                        Close your eyes and imagine a mild version of the situation.
                    </p>

                    <h4>Counter blame response</h4>

                    <p>Imagine someone saying:

                        “This wasn't done correctly.”

                        Notice the tiny defensive reaction.

                        Then mentally practice:

                        “Check before defending.”

                        Relax your body.

                        Imagine yourself saying:

                        “Are you saying that I was responsible for it?”

                        Now you've created an alternative sequence:

                        ambiguous criticism → pause → clarification

                        Do it several times.</p>

                    <h4>Counter scarcity response</h4>

                    <p>Imagine:

                        “I'm short on money this month. Someone who has plenty isn't helping.”

                        Let yourself feel a little of the disappointment.

                        Then:

                        “Scarcity response.”

                        Pause.

                        “Need, not comparison.”

                        Then:

                        “What do I actually need?”

                        And imagine yourself taking one practical action.</p>

                    <p>This is essentially mental rehearsal.

                        You're not merely memorizing words. You're pairing the words with the actual emotional
                        situation.</p>


                </div>
            </section>
            <section class="inner-wrapper">
                <h3> Stage 2 — Practice when I am mildly stressed</h3>
                <img src="images/jesus-judgment.webp" alt="Jesus at Judgment Day">
                <p>This is where many people make a mistake.

                    They practice only while sitting peacefully meditating.

                    Then real life happens and the practice disappears.

                    Instead, deliberately practice during ordinary stress.</p>

                <p>For example:

                    You're doing schoolwork and you're getting frustrated.

                    Stop for 10 seconds:

                    “I'm stressed.”

                    Then:

                    “Check before defending.”

                    or:

                    “Need, not comparison.”

                    Then continue.</p>
                <p>You're teaching your brain:

                    “This response is something I use while stressed.”

                    That's important.</p>

            </section>

            <section class="inner-wrapper">
                <h3>Stage 3 — Catch the OLD response after it has already started</h3>
                <img src="images/jesus-judgment.webp" alt="Jesus at Judgment Day">

                <p>Don't require yourself to catch it before it happens.</p>

                <p>At first you probably won't.

                    You might suddenly realize:

                    “I've been resentfully thinking about this for 20 minutes.”

                    That's not failure.

                    That's actually the training opportunity.</p>

                <p>Say: “There it is. Old pathway.”</p>
                <p>Then: “What do I need right now?”</p>

                <p>Then redirect.</p>

                <p>You're teaching your brain:

                    “Even when the old pathway activates, I can exit it.”

                    That is extremely important.</p>

                <p>You don't need perfect prevention.

                    You need faster recovery.
                </p>

            </section>
            <section class="inner-wrapper">
                <h3>Stage 4 — Practice under genuine stress</h3>
                <img src="images/jesus-judgment.webp" alt="Jesus at Judgment Day">
                <p>Eventually, you want to use the skill when you're:</p>
                <ul class="bullet-list">
                    <li>
                        sleep deprived</li>
                    <li> hungry</li>
                    <li>overloaded with assignments</li>
                    <li>worried about money</li>
                    <li>physically exhausted</li>
                    <li>behind schedule</li>
                    <li>emotionally disappointed</li>
                </ul>
                <p>But don't deliberately overwhelm yourself to practice it.

                    Just use the moments that naturally occur.</p>

                <p>For example:

                    You're exhausted and have three assignments left.</p>

                <p>Old brain:

                    “I can't handle this. Everything is too much.”</p>

                <p>New:

                    “I'm overloaded. I don't need to solve my whole life. What's the next thing that I can do?”</p>

                <p>Then:

                    “Twenty minutes of this assignment. Just focus on that.”</p>

                <p>That's not positive thinking.

                    It's reducing an overwhelming prediction to an actionable present task.

                </p>

            </section>

            <section class="inner-wrapper">
                <h3>There's another extremely important piece: repetition immediately after the trigger</h3>

                <p>If possible, practice the new response within seconds of the old response beginning.

                    Why?

                    Because you're trying to create a new association around the very situation that previously
                    activated the old one.</p>

                <p>The sequence becomes: old cue → old activation → new response</p>

                <p>rather than always practicing: calm state → new response. Just
                    focus on that. That's much more useful.</p>
                <img src="images/esther.webp" alt="Queen Esther painting by Minerva Teichart">

            </section>
            <section class="inner-wrapper">
                <h3>I also need to reward the new pathway</h3>

                <p>After you successfully interrupt an old pattern, notice the success.</p>

                <p>For example:

                    “I was irritated because I thought she was blaming me, but I stopped and asked what she meant.”

                    Then:

                    “That's exactly what I want to become automatic.”</p>

                <p>Or:

                    “I noticed myself comparing my financial situation to theirs, and I redirected toward what I
                    actually need.”</p>

                <p>Pause for a moment and feel the difference.

                    Don't immediately rush on.

                    You're giving your brain a little bit of:

                    “That was successful.”

                    The brain learns not only from the event but from what follows it.</p>
                <img src="images/esther.webp" alt="Queen Esther painting by Minerva Teichart">

            </section>
